{
  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "id": "good-acetal-formation-from-a-hemiacetal-via-an-oxocarbenium",
  "title": "Under acid the hemiacetal of acetaldehyde is protonated, loses water to an oxocarbenium, is attacked by methanol, and is deprotonated to the dimethyl acetal",
  "expect": {
    "kind": "good",
    "note": "The proton transfer heavy fixture. Four steps, three of them move a proton, and every one of those protons has a drawn donor and a drawn acceptor in the same state. This is the case CLAUDE.md's system boundary rule was written for: none of these steps conserves charge on the substrate alone, and a substrate only charge check reports a violation on all three. The load bearing chemistry is step 2. Water does not simply fall off to leave a carbocation; the neighbouring ether oxygen pushes in as it goes, which is why the intermediate is an oxocarbenium with a full octet on carbon rather than an unstabilised primary cation, and why acetal formation needs acid but not a strong one. Drawing step 2 with a single arrow off the C to O bond is the common wrong answer and it is wrong by one arrow, not by one concept."
  },
